---
id: 17467623-0afc-4ec9-a2ff-b2c3807e5673
title: B - The Hero with a Thousand Faces, Joseph Campbell
---

# The characters within the monomyth reflect Jungnian universal archetyes, nested within layers of personal conscious and unconscious material to provide urgency, which breeds variety within a common narrative structure

- Jungnian archetypes are thought to inspire individuals to adventure, embarkment of their Hero's Journey.

- Because these archetypes live within all humans, from the primordial world to the enlightened civilization, the are valid to all of mankind.

- That is to say we all can embark on our own Hero's Journey.

- **Fleeting notes:**

  - Abstraction of imagery, legends, myths, visions and dreams yields a universal storyline, and the universal nature of the narrative causes us to wonder whether it is inherited.
    - There is an indescribable source of appeal, no matter who you are, where you are from, when you are from, it beckons for your attention.
    - And from the study of comparative mythology, Campbell's life works, he realises whether of occidental or oriental origins, theistic or atheistic point of view, monotheistic or polytheistic background, abstraction yields a meta-story, of a meta-hero, and his meta-quest where he/ she encounters meta-antagonists, in search of the meta-boon.
  - And often the Hero's Journey starts with a call for adventure, through the yearning of collective unconscious archetypal images that Jung described.
    - The call to adventure is often manifested through the Wise Old Man, the Spider Mother etc.
      - But these are mere symbols of a deeper meaning, which are the archetypal manifestations of the unconscious, trying to tell consciousness of the need of embarkment.
        - Your unconscious personality is trying to tell you to live out your destiny.
          - To integrate these unconscious contents into consciousness, moving one step closer to the totality of the Self.
      - Myths and dreams hence take a similar tone, and makes use of a similar language, symbols.
        - And this is very different from our conscious, rational language.
          - Why it is so difficult to interpret.
  - But surely personalised details are added to the narrative, which makes it seems like thousands of stories, but in general follows the trail of the meta story.
    - If the premise that myths are perhaps dreams of the primordial world, this explains why there is such great variety, even though they follow a similar narrative arc.
      - Because these symbols originate from our collective unconscious, as Jung might suggest, which is a human prerogative.
        - But to suggest that the creation of symbols through dreams would be the same in each individual would be foolish, as the symbols created are nested within contents of the personalised unconscious or even our consciousness.
          - This is a mere claim, not verified, but possibly the reason of doing so is because it creates a sense of urgency to the dreamer. The dream becomes personal to the individual, and this could be more likely to prompt action.
            - Dreaming of yourself falling to your doom is definitely more likely to impact you than dreaming of some universal mythological motif.

# Destiny in man is manifested by convenient appearance of heralds, suggesting divine intervation that sets the Hero's Journey in motion

- Failure of the royal father to distract his son from setting path to become the eventual Buddha even with a world of wealth, and materialistic joys suggest that destiny could be delayed, but in itself inevitable.

- The convenient appearance of daily human suffering in front of the young Buddha's eyes suggests that once ripe for adventure, there are divine forces that set the Hero's Journey in motion, ensuring the acceptance to the call to adventure.

- **Fleeting notes:**

  - The start of the Hero's Journey occurs when the Hero's destiny finally catches up to him/her, and the call to adventure can no longer be refused.
  - Refusal of the call, initially is always an option. One could always turn away… there is no need to venture into the forest of unknown.
    - Yet the call, on the other hand, will always manifest itself. This is rather similar to the idea of the Enlightment of the young Buddha. His royal father tries to hold him back, throwing him all things materialistic, but it only delayed the inevitable.
    - Every time he steps foot out of his palace, he saw human suffering. He was destined to become the Buddha.
  - And it seems that there are majestic forces that tries to force us to answer the call to adventure, some external force that decides that we are ripe for the adventure.
    - In the story of the Buddha, the sights of human sufferings were in fact signs given from the gods, where the gods descended into the mortal world and revealed to him the human condition.
    - These were counteracting forces to the acts of the royal father. A push and pull tension, and destiny always wins.
  - This reminds me of the time when I started contemplating what learning is, and the idea that I must drastically change of understanding how I learn so that I could be a better student, and hopefully in the future, a better doctor.
    - It was my call to adventure. It is possible for me to refuse it. But this drive towards a goal pushed me through. I had to answer.
    - And once I crossed the threshold, I had to let go of everything I knew. It was terrifying, because I still had obligations in the real world, the most immediate being that I had to at least past my exams.
      - And while passing my exams weren't the overall goal, me answering the call meant that I had to put real obligations in jeopardy.
      - Who's to say that I would bring back something better?
  - Answering the call to adventure as traversing some threshold between the mortal world and the mythological realm, intentionally or accidentally, where the mythological realm is often represented in a myriad of ways: a land far far away, deep in the ocean, or within a dream.
  - And this maybe preceded by daily blunders, this that occur seemingly randomly, but in hindsight as to be a prelude of what's to come.
    - We are largely familiar with the motif that "Good children should never wander into the forest", yet perhaps the appearance of an unexpected animal, the good children chases it and ends up in said forest.
    - There is a magnetic draw to the unknown.
  - Psychologically, it manifests as the unconscious surfacing to the ego, to the conscious.
  - The bubbling up of unconscious symbols to the conscious mind is in fact a call to adventure in the inner world. The symbols it creates manifested into dreams creates the visual aspects that comprises myths and world literature.
  - Or it agrees with the idea of Synchronicity put forth by Jung, that there maybe a meaningful coincidence between the physical world, and the unconscious, a connection between matter and psyche.

# If monsters mirror our inner shadows, avoidance delays but a deeper descent awaits; the fated champion holds Promethean Power to regenerate from monsters domain by mining unmined gold from within

- If monsters mirror our inner shadows, avoidance delays but a deeper descent awaits; the fated champion holds Promethean Power to regenerate from monsters domain by mining unmined gold from within.

- Schisms splitting psyche call not for past solace but present grit facing roots; only death of what was bars new life as individual phoenix emerging.

- If monsters mirror our inner shadows, avoidance delays but a deeper descent awaits; the fated champion holds Promethean Power to regenerate from monsters domain by mining unmined gold from within.

- This sentence speaks a profound truth through poetic lens - that only in meeting life's grimaces fully formed can one be reforged oneself. For as long as past personae persist in their clinging convictions of persisting perfection, any new becoming stands barred from breaching birth.

- Yet when mirror-monsters are met with mettle unflinching, the former figuration yields to fate's fiery dissolution. Where dread doppelgangers once loomed, naught remains but dust of yore's husk. ****From these ashes, unconstrained by any carbon-copy of convictions past, the soul stands reborn - not as mythic phoenix fated to repeat, but as singular self surfaced solely by strength to seize life's lessons, flames now feathers of freedom to define its destined, due departure.**** Thus comes newness into being as old orders shatter - an individual imago emerges where shadows collapsed, self-shatterer yet self-realized through facing fearlessness with fettered courage. This truth the poem paints through metaphor's magic - that only in confronting fears materialized can we be midwifed anew.

- **Fleeting notes:**

  - Monsters are avatars we see often in mythology, are often mirrors of our infantile fixation, the effect of the un-individualised unconscious manifesting into behaviour.
    - It's source of power stems from the mythic realm, from dreams, from the unconscious.
      - Never when we are fully conscious, logical, awake, do we face the abomination that is ourself.
        - When awake, we rarely realise the full life potentials that we carry within ourselves forever, that we are unable to bring into adulthood.
          - Hence the first step to slain the tyrant-monster is often detachment. Detachment, or withdrawal of emphasis implies a radical transfer by which we see ourselves, from the external to the internal, from the macrocosm to the microcosm, from only seeing reflections on the mirror to coming face to face with the beast. From merely living in the ordinary world, and crossing the threshold to the mystical world.
    - These are primal, archaic instincts, emotions, those that insight fear, confusion. They are parts of us that we do not understand nor accept.
      - Often manifesting in dreams as demons from a specific culture, these are the difficulties the hero must encounter, and once and for all, battle and eradicate these demons, and breakthrough to the undistorted, pure archetypal images.
  - While the tyrant-monster is often slained by knight in shining armour, representing light, promise, change and resurrection of the world, it is also possible for regeneration to be sought from within the monster's cave/ tyrant's empire itself: For the monster's domain carry not only the infantile fixations, but all the potential that we were unable to carry into adult realisation. The golden seed never dies.
    - We all have to be Prometheus for ourselves. We are capable agents of our own transformation. Confrontation of what lies within brings forth new insight, new knowledge, and new growth.
      - The Hero's Deed can, in fact be performed by anybody. It follows the similar natural arc of withdrawal from the world scene and entry into the depths of the psyche.
        - Then to realise these difficulties and give battle to the tyrant-monster. In hopes to encounter, and return with the boon.

# The Great Mother represents the totality of the known, the unity of duality, and symbolises the natural orders of the indifferent cosmos, where the Heroic deed may involve the intaking of the total image of the Great Mother

- The Great Mother not only encompasses life, but also death: She is the "womb", but also the "tomb".

- Taking in the image of the Great Mother entails the recognition of the union of the good and the bad, and realising that this is the universal order.

- The hero comes to recognise the totality of what could be known, through the meeting of the goddess. This involves the necessary purge of infantile, biased opinions of duality.

- **Fleeting notes:**

  - Similar to other mythological motif, the idea of Women, personified as The Great Mother, the Spider Women, the Universal Mother, pertains, at first glance, duality of qualities - The good: nurturing, protective, warmth, beauty, and the bad: absent, forbidding.
    - To the naked eye, the archetype of the Great Mother is often associated with the positive aspects of feminism: Protective, Nurturing, Beautiful.
      - But the same archetype also encompasses the negative aspects of feminism: Controlling, forbidding, absence.
    - But the Great Mother is more than just a symbol of feminism, it is a symbol of unity, of creation, transformation and destruction.
  - From the cosmic view, Women represents the totality of what could be known, the unity of duality.
    - But as in mythology, Women is represented as the unity of duality, of light and dark, of life and death, male and female energies, of Heaven and Hell.
    - Hence, the "Goddess" in mythology often draws parallels to the natural world, or in the grander scheme of things, the totality of existence, the positive and negative aspects of life.
      - The Great Mother is truly seen when one takes in her life-giving, yet world destroying aspects.
  - Only the Hero who can truly see who the Goddess is be able to attain what is unknown, and is often signified by a sacred marriage.
    - The Great Mother motif can be seen in the mythological realm, the realm of unknown, separated from the mortal world by the veil of duality.
      - Mere mortals carry a partial view of the Great Mother, either only seeing the positive aspects, or only seeing the negative aspects.
    - Only the initiated, destined Hero who crosses the first threshold can take in the image of totality of the Great Mother, which deepens the understanding of the world.
      - One of the function of the Hero's Journey is to achieve such understanding of the world.
        - Breaking beyond the limitations of duality, the Hero confronts the Great Mother and achieves understanding of unity, of harmony.
          - The opposing qualities are one and the same.
            - This is true wisdom.
      - To some extent, we living in the enlightened world are already aware of the unity of duality.
        - We merely sometimes slip back in to old ways of thinking.
          - The Hero's Journey in this sense remains necessary in contemporary life, but in this rendition, it is not the discovery of the totality of the world, but the re-discovery of the totality of the world.
            - It is not the acquaintance with the Goddess, but the re-acquaintance with the Goddess.

# Intertwined, tragedy and comedy are eternally bound, and when the world redeemer bestows such realisation, tragedy no longer predominates, for where horror persists, they can be met by all-encompassing love

- The common mythological motif of tragedy and comedy is similar to what is commonly depicted in children's movies and fairytales: There is happiness, and then there is sadness.

- But this tension between the two seemingly polar opposite themes are two parts of the same coin, and it is often the world-redeemer's deed to bring forth its realisations.

- And for that: 'Happy families are all alike; and every unhappy family is unhappy in its own way' said Tolstoy. All redeemed world is alike.

- **Fleeting notes:**

  - Tragedy is often contrasted with comedy as polar opposites of the human condition. The former is intimate, realistic. We see tragedy every day, as if suffering is the only constant of the cosmos, of our very fate. This is in contrast with comedy, a make-belief fairy tale that cannot be taken seriously, taken only in small doses for escape, but we know it to be detached from reality.
    - Tragedy is constant. Death is tragic. Heartbreak is tragic. It is usually thought as the default tone by which life sets. Even few success stories are temporary, because it could be taken away at an instance.
      - Comedy should only be taken in small doses. For they could very much be the preceding of a tragic story, it is not thought as the eternal truth.
    - But how why are we capable of realising that comedy is not representative of life itself, when the same can be said about tragedy?
  - Tragedy is often associated with a tragic-ending, and comedy associated with a happy one. But a comedic tale is not read in contradiction of a tragic one, but read as the transcendence of the universal tragedy of man. It's all about perspective.
    - The objective world remains what it is, for the world can be thought as merely as a place of things. But when the emphasis within the subject (subjective experience) is shifted, the tone of the story could be entirely transformed.
      - And perhaps the mystic ways by which we can seemingly transform the world is forever nested in the depths of the art of narration, it is in fact part of the Hero's deed, to reach into the depths of the uncounscious and realise that tragedy and comedy is eternally bound.
        - And when this boon is returned, tragedy is no longer ubiquitous, and even in the face of persistent horrors, they can be met with all-sustaining love and compassion.
  - The two are intertwined, eternally bound as the same mythological motif, that constitutes the realisation of the totality of life.
